ith pytest.raises(QueueEmpty): q.get_nowait().\n\nAlso _consume_expired: put with timeout that expires adds to _putters with timeout... Actually expired putters: put with timeout creates a future with a timeout callback; when it expires, the putter is removed via _consume_expired. Not strictly needed for lines 262-272 but line 262 is _consume_expired call \u2014 it executes regardless. Fine.\n\nCleanup: no global state; Queue objects are local. No IOLoop needed since put is synchronous in the full case. But is `put` a coroutine? In modern tornado, `put` is `async def`? Let me check: in tornado 6, `put` is:\n\n```python\ndef put(self, item, timeout=None) -> Future[None]:\n    future = Future()\n    ...\n    return future\n```\n\nActually in tornado 6.x queues.py:\n\n```python\ndef put(\n    self, item: _T, timeout: Optional[Union[float, datetime.timedelta]] = None\n) -> \"Future[None]\":\n    \"\"\"Put an item into the queue, possibly waiting for room to do so.\n    ...\n    \"\"\"\n    future = Future()  # type: Future[None]\n    if not self.full():\n        self.__put_internal(item)\n        future_set_result_unless_cancelled(future, None)\n    elif timeout is None:\n        self._putters.append((item, future))\n        self._finished.clear()\n    else:\n        timeout_handle = ioloop.IOLoop.current().call_later(\n            self._timeout_timeout(timeout), ...\n        )\n        ...\n    return future\n```\n\nHmm, I'm not 100% sure. In older tornado (5.x) it was a generator. In tornado 6, `put` returns a Future and is not a coroutine \u2014 calling it on a full queue with timeout=None appends to _putters synchronously. Let me verify with get_info on put.",
